hey this is presh talwalkar  on the morning of january 1st a hospital  nursery has three boys and some number  of girls  that night a woman gives birth to a  child and the child is placed in the  nursery  on january 2nd a statistician conducts a  survey and selects a child at random  from the nursery  the survey includes the newborn child  and every child in the nursery from  january 1st  the randomly selected child is a boy  what is the probability the child born  on january 1st was a boy  this problem has been asked as an  interview question for technical jobs  can you figure it out  give this problem a try and when you're  ready keep watching the video for the  solution  i'll get started with the common  misconception  the answer is not 50  while it is true that there is an equal  50 chance that the newborn child is a  boy or a girl  that is not the probability you're  looking for  the question is asking for the  probability of a newborn boy  given that the statistician randomly  picked a boy  how is this different  imagine the nursery started with zero  boys  if the statistician randomly picked a  boy  you would know the newborn must have  been a boy  that would be a hundred percent  probability  in general  because the statistician randomly  selected a boy  it is more likely that the newborn child  was a boy  intuitively the probability will be  greater than 50 percent  now let's calculate it and see what it  is  the problem does not specify the number  of girls at the beginning but we'll  assume three girls and see where this  calculation goes  so we start out with three girls and  three boys  there's is a 50 chance that the newborn  child is a boy  this makes for three girls and four boys  there's also a 50 chance that the  newborn is a girl which makes for four  girls and three boys  so what's the probability of selecting a  boy in each case  in the top range there are four boys out  of seven children total  this is a four out of seven chance and  we multiply that by the 50 chance of  being in this branch of having a boy  this is the probability of selecting a  boy and having the newborn child be a  boy  in the bottom branch there are three  boys out of seven children  and we need to multiply by the 50 chance  that the child born was a girl  this is the probability of selecting a  boy and having the newborn child be a  girl  so what's the probability of selecting a  boy this is the sum of these two  probabilities  this will be equal to three and a half  divided by seven and this makes sense  intuitively we're either going to have  three or four boys so on average we have  three and a half boys and we're always  going to have seven children so we have  three and a half out of seven and that's  a 50 chance  so what's the probability we want to  calculate  we want to know what's the probability  of having a newborn child given that we  selected a boy  this is the probability of the top  branch divided by the sum of the two  probabilities of the two branches in  other words it's the probability of  selecting a boy and have it be a newborn  boy  and  divided by the probability of selecting  a boy  so we take the ratio of the probability  of the top range divided by the sum of  the two probabilities  this simplifies to 4 out of 7 which is  approximately  57.1 percent so this is larger than 50  as we expected  now what would happen we assumed three  girls in this case but what would happen  in general  let's assume we have g girls  we start out with g girls and three boys  there's a 50 chance we have a boy  which would result in g girls and four  boys  there's a 50 chance we have a girl which  results in g plus one girls and three  boys  in the top branch there's a four divided  by g plus four chance of selecting a boy  and we multiply that by the fifty  percent chance of the top branch having  a boy  this is the probability of selecting a  boy and have it be a newborn boy  in the bottom branch we have three boys  divided by g plus four children in total  we multiply by the 50 probability of the  branch this is the probability of  selecting a boy and having a newborn  girl  so what's the probability of selecting a  boy this is the sum of the two  probabilities of these two branches  this is equal to three point five  divided by g plus four  intuitively we either have three or four  boys the next day happening with equal  chance so we have three and a half boys  on average and we divide that by the  total number of children which will  always be g plus four  so what's the probability that we want  to calculate the probability of having a  newborn boy given that we selected a boy  at random  this is the ratio of the top branch  divided by the sum of the two branches  so we put in these two  probabilities and you can see that the g  plus 4 actually cancels out  this simplifies to be 4 over 7 which is  approximately  57.1 percent  so the two counter-intuitive things  going on in this problem one is that it  doesn't matter the number of girls we  start with and two the probability is  more than fifty percent  we can actually generalize the problem  one more step we start out with g girls  but let's assume we have b boys instead  of saying we have three boys let's say  we have b boys  in that case we'll go ahead and draw out  our tree  we'll count the number of boys and girls  we'll calculate the conditional  probabilities in each of these cases  we again calculate the probability of  selecting a boy  and then we calculate the probability of  having a newborn boy given that we  selected a boy  this all simplifies to be b plus one  over two b plus one  so as the number of boys increases  this ratio is going to approach one half  which is the 50 percent that we would  expect  so for example if you started out with  50 boys  then you would have an approximately  50.5 percent chance that the newborn  child was a boy if you selected it so  that's pretty close to 50 it's slightly  more  but it's a very counterintuitive problem  it doesn't matter the number of girls  you start with and the probability is  more than 50 percent  did you figure this out thanks for  watching this video please subscribe to  my channel i make videos on math and  game theory you can catch me on my blog  mind your decisions which you can follow  on facebook google plus and patreon you  can catch me on social media at  preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books there  are links in the video description

